### Formal Definition of Set Algebra in Java Collections

The `java.util.Set` interface provides a comprehensive implementation of classical mathematical set theory through bulk collection mutating methods:

1. **Union ($A \cup B$)**: Combines all elements from both sets without duplicates.
   ```java
   Set<T> union = new HashSet<>(setA);
   union.addAll(setB); // Result contains all elements in A or B
   ```

2. **Intersection ($A \cap B$)**: Retains only elements present in **both** sets.
   ```java
   Set<T> intersection = new HashSet<>(setA);
   intersection.retainAll(setB); // Result contains elements in A AND B
   ```

3. **Relative Complement / Difference ($A \setminus B$)**: Removes all elements from $A$ that appear in $B$.
   ```java
   Set<T> difference = new HashSet<>(setA);
   difference.removeAll(setB); // Result contains elements in A but NOT B
   ```

4. **Subset Verification ($A \subseteq B$)**: Verifies whether all elements of $A$ exist in $B$.
   ```java
   boolean isSubset = setB.containsAll(setA);
   ```

---

### Defensive Copying: Preventing In-Place State Destruction

A critical architectural pitfall in Java is forgetting that `addAll()`, `retainAll()`, and `removeAll()` are **destructive mutating methods**:
* Calling `setA.retainAll(setB)` directly alters `setA` permanently.
* In clean code, always instantiate a new collection (e.g. `new HashSet<>(setA)`) to receive the results of set algebra, leaving the original collections intact.

---

### Executable Demonstration: Enterprise Customer Segmentation

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class SetAlgebraAnalyticsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> newsletterSubscribers = Set.of("alice@corp.com", "bob@corp.com", "carol@corp.com");
        Set<String> payingCustomers = Set.of("bob@corp.com", "carol@corp.com", "david@corp.com");

        // 1. Union (Total Unique Reach)
        Set<String> totalReach = new HashSet<>(newsletterSubscribers);
        totalReach.addAll(payingCustomers);
        System.out.println("1. Union (Total Reach):         " + totalReach);

        // 2. Intersection (Subscribers Who Are Also Paying Customers)
        Set<String> subscriberBuyers = new HashSet<>(newsletterSubscribers);
        subscriberBuyers.retainAll(payingCustomers);
        System.out.println("2. Intersection (Sub & Paid):    " + subscriberBuyers);

        // 3. Difference (Subscribers Who Have NOT Paid)
        Set<String> prospectsOnly = new HashSet<>(newsletterSubscribers);
        prospectsOnly.removeAll(payingCustomers);
        System.out.println("3. Difference (Prospects Only):  " + prospectsOnly);

        // 4. Symmetric Difference (Exclusive to either A or B, but not both)
        Set<String> symmetricDiff = new HashSet<>(totalReach);
        symmetricDiff.removeAll(subscriberBuyers);
        System.out.println("4. Symmetric Difference:         " + symmetricDiff);
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
1. Union (Total Reach):         [david@corp.com, alice@corp.com, carol@corp.com, bob@corp.com]
2. Intersection (Sub & Paid):    [carol@corp.com, bob@corp.com]
3. Difference (Prospects Only):  [alice@corp.com]
4. Symmetric Difference:         [david@corp.com, alice@corp.com]
```

---

### Summary of Set Algebraic Methods

| Mathematical Term | Notation | Java Set API | In-Place Mutation? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Union** | $A \cup B$ | `setA.addAll(setB)` | Modifies `setA` |
| **Intersection** | $A \cap B$ | `setA.retainAll(setB)` | Modifies `setA` |
| **Difference** | $A \setminus B$ | `setA.removeAll(setB)` | Modifies `setA` |
| **Subset Test** | $A \subseteq B$ | `setB.containsAll(setA)` | Read-only boolean |

